---
/**
 * docs/mcp.astro — how to connect an MCP client to Chroma (D-255).
 *
 * Every command, path, port, tool name and error string on this page is
 * transcribed from real repo sources: mcp/server.py, mcp/README.md,
 * app/src-tauri/src/chroma/control.rs's documented contract, and
 * docs/notes/mcp-tool-coverage.md. Nothing here is an invented API shape.
 */
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { TOOL_GROUPS, TOOL_TOTALS, MOTION_GAP, CONNECTION, VERIFIED_ON } from '../../data/mcp.ts';

const sections = [
  { id: 'how', label: 'How it works' },
  { id: 'install', label: 'Install' },
  { id: 'connect', label: 'Connect a client' },
  { id: 'first', label: 'First calls' },
  { id: 'tools', label: 'The tools' },
  { id: 'http', label: 'The HTTP surface' },
  { id: 'trouble', label: 'Troubleshooting' },
];
---

<Base
  title="Connect an agent to Chroma — MCP setup"
  description="Real setup instructions for driving Chroma from Claude Code or any MCP client: install the stdio server, connect it, and call the 106 shipped tools across the editor and the colorist."
>
  <article class="doc">
    <header class="shell hd">
      <p class="eyebrow">Documentation</p>
      <h1>Connect an agent</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Chroma ships a stdio MCP server. Point any MCP client at it and that client can drive the
        running desktop app — {TOOL_TOTALS.shipped} tools across the editor and the colorist, every
        one of them going through the same store action the GUI button calls.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="shell layout">
      <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
        <p class="eyebrow">On this page</p>
        {sections.map((s) => <a href={`#${s.id}`}>{s.label}</a>)}
      </nav>

      <div class="prose">
        <section id="how">
          <h2>How it works</h2>
          <p>
            The server itself holds no grading or editing logic. Each tool posts one op to a small
            HTTP control server running inside the app, which forwards it to the frontend, which
            applies it through the identical store action a button press would. That is why an
            agent's edit moves real sliders, lands on the same undo stack, and why reading state
            back reflects whatever you changed by hand a moment ago.
          </p>
          <pre class="mono diagram"><code>Claude (MCP client)
  │  stdio
{CONNECTION.serverPath}
  │  HTTP  POST /op {`{op, args}`}
{CONNECTION.controlServerPath}   (control server, in the app, port {CONNECTION.defaultPort})
  │  Tauri events
app/src/hooks/useChromaControl.ts   (frontend — owns the state)
  │  the SAME store actions the GUI buttons call
render  →  {`{ rendered frame, histogram, adjustments }`}</code></pre>
          <p class="note">
            Mutating Colorist tools return the re-rendered frame, its histogram and a compact scope
            summary — the new measurement, for free, after the change.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="install">
          <h2>Install</h2>
          <p>From a clone of the repository:</p>
          <pre class="mono"><code>cd mcp
python3 -m venv .venv
.venv/bin/pip install -r requirements.txt</code></pre>
          <p>
            You also need the Chroma desktop app running. The control server binds when the app
            starts, and the bridge is live while a project is open.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="connect">
          <h2>Connect a client</h2>
          <h3>Claude Code</h3>
          <pre class="mono"><code>claude mcp add chroma -- \
  /ABS/PATH/chroma/mcp/.venv/bin/python \
  /ABS/PATH/chroma/mcp/server.py</code></pre>

          <h3>Any client that takes a JSON config</h3>
          <p>
            The server is an ordinary stdio MCP server, so the standard client config shape works
            unchanged — the command is the virtualenv's Python, the argument is the server file:
          </p>
          <pre class="mono"><code>{`{
  "mcpServers": {
    "chroma": {
      "command": "/ABS/PATH/chroma/mcp/.venv/bin/python",
      "args": ["/ABS/PATH/chroma/mcp/server.py"]
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>

          <h3>A non-default port</h3>
          <p>
            The control server listens on <code class="mono">{CONNECTION.defaultPort}</code> unless
            you override it. If you changed it on the app side, set the same value in the server's
            environment:
          </p>
          <pre class="mono"><code>{CONNECTION.portEnvVar}=19999</code></pre>
        </section>

        <section id="first">
          <h2>First calls</h2>
          <p>
            Two calls are worth making before anything else, and both are cheap:
          </p>
          <dl class="defs">
            <dt class="mono">get_state</dt>
            <dd>
              What is loaded, the current primary adjustments, a mask summary, the multi-shot
              session and the open project. It reflects manual edits, so it is also how you check
              what the human just did.
            </dd>
            <dt class="mono">editor_get_capabilities</dt>
            <dd>
              Static reference data with no round trip to the app — the non-obvious compositing
              rules, the per-property rule that a keyframe silently beats a transform written for
              the same property, and the known rough edges. Read it once per session before your
              first transform, keyframe or export.
            </dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <section id="tools">
          <h2>The tools</h2>
          <p>
            Counted directly from <code class="mono">mcp/server.py</code> on {VERIFIED_ON}:
            {TOOL_TOTALS.all} tool definitions exist, of which {TOOL_TOTALS.debugOnly} are
            development-only and compiled out of a production build, leaving
            {' '}{TOOL_TOTALS.shipped} that ship.
          </p>

          {
            TOOL_GROUPS.map((g) => (
              <div class="grp">
                <h3>
                  {g.name} <span class="cnt mono" style={`--tone:var(--marker-${g.signal})`}>{g.count}</span>
                </h3>
                <p>{g.blurb}</p>
                <ul class="names mono">
                  {g.sample.map((n) => (
                    <li>{n}</li>
                  ))}
                </ul>
              </div>
            ))
          }

          <div class="grp">
            <h3>
              Motion <span class="cnt mono zero">{MOTION_GAP.count}</span>
            </h3>
            <p>{MOTION_GAP.note}</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="http">
          <h2>The HTTP surface</h2>
          <p>
            You do not need this to use the MCP server, but it is what the server talks to and it
            is worth knowing it exists. The control server accepts one endpoint:
          </p>
          <pre class="mono"><code>POST http://127.0.0.1:{CONNECTION.defaultPort}/op
Content-Type: application/json

{`{ "op": "get_state", "args": {} }`}</code></pre>
          <p>
            It is bound to loopback only. A request that the app cannot answer within its bridge
            timeout comes back as a <code class="mono">504</code>, which the MCP server turns into a
            readable error rather than a hang — long-running work (a depth track, a transcript, a
            video analysis) is always start-then-poll for exactly this reason.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="trouble">
          <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
          <dl class="defs">
            <dt>“Cannot reach the Chroma control server”</dt>
            <dd>
              The app is not running, or it is on another port. Start it, or set
              <code class="mono"> {CONNECTION.portEnvVar}</code> to match.
            </dd>
            <dt>“The Chroma app did not respond”</dt>
            <dd>
              A 504 from the bridge: the window is closed, or nothing is open. The bridge only runs
              while the editor view is mounted.
            </dd>
            <dt>A response with a histogram but no image</dt>
            <dd>
              A known limitation, not a failure. The app renders to a native GPU surface, so the
              returned frame is a best-effort headless re-render. The histogram, scopes and
              adjustments are always accurate.
            </dd>
          </dl>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
